    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule: **creating an owner is rejected with 409 when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners.**\n\nFollowing the existing conventions in this Spring Boot app (the same pattern used for `DuplicateOwnerTelephoneException` and `DuplicateOwnerHouseholdException`), I made three changes:\n\n1. **New exception** \u2014 `rest/advice/OwnerCityAtCapacityException.java`, a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending city.\n\n2. **Exception handler** \u2014 added `handleOwnerCityAtCapacityException` to `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, which maps the exception to a **409 Conflict** `ProblemDetail` with `errors: [\"city\"]`, mirroring the other duplicate handlers.\n\n3. **The check** \u2014 in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, added a `rejectCityAtCapacity(...)` step (after the duplicate-telephone/household checks, before persistence). It counts existing owners in the same city using a case-insensitive match on `city` \u2014 consistent with how `assignCustomerCode` already groups a city \u2014 and throws when the count is `>= 50` (`MAX_OWNERS_PER_CITY`).\n\nThe acceptance test `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fills a fresh city to exactly 50, then expects the 51st to be rejected) passes, and `BUILD SUCCESS` with `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
